{
  "title": "Galeazzi Fractures",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/trauma/1029/galeazzi-fractures",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Jan 24 2026",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "A galeazzi fracture is a distal 1/3 radial shaft fracture with an associated distal radioulnar joint (DRUJ) injury.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis can be suspected with a distal radius fracture with widening of the radioulnar joint on AP wrist radiographs and volar/dorsal subluxation of the radioulnar joint on lateral wrist radiographs.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is generally ORIF of the distal radius followed by assessing the stability of the DRUJ which may be warrant subsequent immobilization, DRUJ pinning or ORIF of the DRUJ.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence of DRUJ instability",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "if radial fracture is <7.5 cm from articular surface",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "unstable in 55%",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "if radial fracture is >7.5 cm from articular surface",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "unstable in 6%",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Mechanism",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "direct wrist trauma",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "typically dorsolateral aspect",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "fall onto outstretched hand with forearm in pronation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "DRUJ",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "sigmoid notch",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "found along ulnar border of distal radius",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "is a shallow concavity for the articulating ulnar head",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**volar and dorsal radioulnar ligaments**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/1029/images/druj.jpg",
                  "caption": "Shows the distal radioulnar articulation"
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "function as the primary stabilizers of the DRUJ",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "most stable in **supination**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
